
; ======================================================================
;  hw_addr.pi4 - the Pi 4's answer to "is this address safe to touch".
;
;  The core asks HwAddrCheck() before every command that takes an address
;  from the operator (Anvil/Core/addrsafe.pbi, and the contract in
;  Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi). ON THIS BOARD THE ANSWER IS ALWAYS SAFE, and this
;  file exists to say so deliberately rather than by omission.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY SAFE FOR EVERYTHING, AND WHAT THAT CLAIM IS AND IS NOT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The seam exists because on the Arduino UNO Q's QCM2290 an MMIO access
;  to a clock-gated block STALLS THE INTERCONNECT - the instruction never
;  retires and the board needs its power removing. THE BCM2711 DOES NOT
;  BEHAVE THAT WAY, and that is a measured difference and not an
;  assumption about it:
;
;    * Every peripheral in the $FE000000 window is behind clocks the
;      firmware has already started before Anvil is entered. `memory
;      FE201000 20` reading the PL011 is how the mini-UART routing bug
;      was cornered, and that read is in this monitor's own history as a
;      thing that WORKED - see the note at CmdMem in Anvil/Core/memcmd.pbi.
;    * The V3D, DMA, mailbox, GENET, PCIe/xHCI, EMMC and SDIO register
;      blocks are all read by this monitor's own drivers, at this
;      exception level, with no special preparation beyond what those
;      drivers do. The register windows answer.
;    * DRAM answers everywhere. `mtest` writes and reads it back.
;    * An address with NOTHING behind it reads as a value and does not
;      hang. It is a wrong answer rather than a hang, and the monitor has
;      always said out loud that reading such an address is the
;      operator's risk to take.
;
;  SO THE CLAIM IS NARROW AND IT IS TRUE: no address on this board is
;  known to stall the bus. It is NOT the claim that every address holds
;  something useful, and it is NOT the claim that a write anywhere is a
;  good idea - the monitor's own window refusals (HitsMonitor,
;  InPayload) are unchanged and still run, because they answer a
;  completely different question: not "will this access complete" but
;  "will it destroy the thing that is running".
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY THIS IS A FILE AND NOT THREE LINES AT THE FOOT OF THE MEMORY MAP
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Two reasons, and the second is the load-bearing one.
;
;  It needs #HW_ADDR_SAFE, which is defined in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi, and
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4 is included BEFORE hal.pi4 by
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4BootContainerSelfTest.pi4. Putting
;  the answer in the memory map would break that diagnostic's build for
;  no reason at all.
;
;  And a board that answers "everything is fine" should have to write
;  that down somewhere a reader will find it. An empty implementation is
;  indistinguishable from a forgotten one, and the next person to bring
;  up a third target will read this file to find out what the seam is
;  for. If the Pi 4 ever grows a block that has to be voted for - a
;  power-domain the firmware leaves collapsed, say - the table goes here
;  and nothing else in the tree changes.
; ======================================================================

; NOTHING MATCHED, SO THERE IS NOTHING TO REPORT. These three exist
; because hal.pi4 defines them as part of the seam and a board must
; supply the whole seam, not the parts it happens to use. They are only
; ever read after an answer that was not SAFE, which cannot happen here.
Procedure.i HwAddrReason()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwAddrBlockLo()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwAddrBlockHi()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwAddrCheck(lo, hi, forWrite) - the Pi 4's answer.
;
;  THE ARGUMENTS ARE ACCEPTED AND NOT USED, and that is the honest shape
;  for this board. Naming them keeps the seam's signature identical
;  across boards, which is what makes a backend a swap rather than a
;  rewrite, and it puts the reader of THIS file in front of the exact
;  question the other board answers differently.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwAddrCheck(lo.i, hi.i, forWrite.i)
  ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_SAFE
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwAddrReport() - what `info` prints about this board's answer.
;
;  Every board prints its own table here, with where each row came from.
;  This one has no table, so it says that, and says why - a blank section
;  under a heading in `info` reads as a monitor that failed to fill it in.
;
;  AND IT PRINTS WHERE THE MONITOR IS, because the sentence below sends
;  the reader straight to "the monitor's own window guards" and there was
;  no way to ask what those windows are. Two questions meet in this
;  section and they are not the same one: HwAddrCheck answers "will this
;  access complete" - here, always yes - and the monitor window answers
;  "will this access destroy the thing that is running", which is the one
;  that still refuses writes on this board. The extent was reachable only
;  by tripping over it: aim a write inside the monitor and the refusal
;  names the window. A number an operator can only find by hitting it is
;  not reported, it is hidden.
;
;  IT ASKS HwMonLo()/HwMonHi() RATHER THAN READING THE CONSTANTS, even
;  though on this board they ARE the constants. Those two procedures are
;  what the overlap test itself asks, so this prints the answer that will
;  actually be used - and a board that told `info` one thing and the
;  guard another would then have to be made to disagree on purpose,
;  rather than by an oversight in one of them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwAddrReport()
  ; WHERE THE MONITOR IS. First, because it is the concrete answer and
  ; the prose below it explains a refusal that never happens here.
  ; EVERY REGION, THROUGH THE ONE PRINTER THE MAP HAS - 2026-09-08.
  ; This block used to print the code window and the data window from
  ; constants and then say the pair "come from COMPILE-TIME CONSTANTS on
  ; this board and cannot be wrong". That sentence was true of the data
  ; window and FALSE of the code window from the moment the monitor
  ; stopped having a fixed top: its extent is now the size of the image
  ; the firmware loaded, measured by the running program. A section of
  ; `info` that explained the old certainty would have been the most
  ; confident wrong paragraph in the monitor.
  PutMonitorMap()
  PrintN("            A write into any of that is refused and the refusal names")
  PrintN("            the region. The first line is MEASURED, not declared: the")
  PrintN("            image carries the address of its own end, so the monitor")
  PrintN("            reserves exactly what it is and there is no maximum size.")
  PrintN("            Type map for the same numbers with the payload windows.")
  PrintN("addresses   every address on this board answers SAFE, so the commands")
  PrintN("            that take an address from you are refused by nothing but")
  PrintN("            the monitor's own window guards, exactly as they always")
  PrintN("            have been here. Nothing on this board is known to stall")
  PrintN("            the bus: the peripheral window is clocked by the firmware")
  PrintN("            before this monitor is entered, DRAM answers everywhere,")
  PrintN("            and an address with nothing behind it reads a value")
  PrintN("            rather than hanging. Anvil asks the question because its")
  PrintN("            other target is not like this - there, one read of a")
  PrintN("            gated block stops the processor until the power is")
  PrintN("            pulled - and one core asks every board the same thing.")
EndProcedure
